@page
@model Htmxmas.Pages.Samples.Day_07.IndexModel

@{
  ViewData["Title"] = "Gift Search As You Type";
}

<div class="row">
  <div class="col-6">
    <markdown>
      # Day 7: Gift Search As You Type
      
      Users have come to expect search experiences that are fast, responsive, and intuitive. The search-as-you-type experience is a great way to provide this.
      This sample demonstrates how easy it can be to add this functionality to your application.
      
      ## How Does This Work?
      
      You can look at the code on this page, but in general, we listen for `keyup` on the input, but only when its value has `changed`, with a delay of `500ms`.
      The trigger lives directly on the input, which keeps it valid in htmx 4. When it fires, we make a request to the server with the
      search criteria. The server then returns a list of results. We then swap the contents of our search results
      container with the results.
      
      The input sends its own value with the request. Need more than what the user types, like radio buttons or checkboxes?
      Add an `hx-include` pointing at the surrounding form (or any other fields).
    </markdown>
    
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Paging", new PagingModel(7))
  </div>
  <div class="col-6">
    <h2 class="text-center">Search For The Perfect Gift</h2>
    
    <form method="get" asp-page="Index">
      <div class="input-group mb-3">
        <input type="text" asp-for="Search"
               hx-get hxn-page="Index"
               hx-trigger="keyup changed delay:500ms"
               hx-target="#search-results"
               hx-swap="outerHTML"
               class="form-control"
               placeholder="Search..."
               aria-label="search criteria">
        <div class="input-group-append">
          <button class="btn btn-outline-danger" type="submit">Search</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </form>

    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Results")

  </div>


</div>
